## My best guess at the mechanism (unconfirmed)

`ink`'s Yoga layout tree (`yoga-layout`, compiled from C++ via Emscripten/embind) requires explicit `.free()`/`.freeRecursive()` on its native nodes — WASM linear memory isn't garbage-collected by V8. If Ink's unmount path isn't freeing every Yoga node it created (perhaps specifically ones touched by a `setRawMode` toggle mid-render, since that's the one variable I could isolate that makes the difference), that would produce exactly this signature: `external` grows, survives `global.gc()`, and stays confined to whatever `external` tracks rather than `heapUsed`/`arrayBuffers`. I want to be upfront that I have not confirmed this specifically — I wasn't able to build a reduced case that isolates Yoga from the rest of Ink, and a simplified rewrite of the same component shape did *not* reproduce it, so there may be another variable I'm missing. Reporting the `external`-vs-`heapUsed` evidence and the working (if unwieldy) repro in case it's useful, rather than sitting on it while chasing a fully minimal case further.

## Workaround we're using

Since `node --test` spawns one child process per test *file*, splitting a large component-test file into one file per component keeps each file's total `render()`-calling test count low, which empirically avoids the growth ever reaching a dangerous size. Not a fix, just containment.

